              "tool_use_id": "toolu_01NjfPLrN94UFSPa58Dw1hW7",
              "type": "tool_result",
              "content": "1\t\\begin{abstract}\n2\tLLM agent routing---deciding when to rely on internal reasoning versus external computation---is typically addressed by training routers directly, without first diagnosing what signal drives the routing preference. We introduce an episode-level counterfactual framework that decomposes routing signal into mechanistic components by comparing pure-strategy outcomes \\emph{before} any router is built. Applied to ScienceWorld, the framework reveals that 78.9\\% of non-tie routing labels arise from a single mechanism: \\emph{death asymmetry}, where internal reasoning triggers catastrophic failure while the deterministic strategy survives. This pattern replicates across three model families spanning 7B to 70B parameters; at larger scale, death avoidance accounts for the entire performance gap between strategies. Always-deterministic matches 96.3\\% of oracle routing decisions, leaving negligible room for learned step-level routing. Boundary probes on MATH and ALFWorld confirm the framework distinguishes qualitatively different routing mechanisms---including environments where no routing signal exists\\footnote{Code and data are available at \\url{https://anonymous.4open.science/r/simgate-modular-routing-ED12/}.}.\n3\t\\end{abstract}\n4\t"
            }
          ],
